### 2026-08-17T02:15:25.661Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

Primary for **note entry and editing** — i.e., which interaction surface is the main way you actually build/change a composition, as opposed to just displaying it.

Concretely, Compose currently supports these actions entirely through Tonnetz-tap gestures:
- **Add a note**: tap a hex cell (records it into `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]`)
- **Select notes**: tap/drag-select on the lattice
- **Delete selected**: a button, acting on the tap-based selection
- **Insert**: insert a note after the current selection
- **Translate**: drag the selection to a new position (shifts pitch/time)
- **Rotate**: rotate the selection around a pivot

The open question is whether *those same actions* — add, select, delete, insert, move, rotate — stay driven by tapping/dragging on the hex Tonnetz (with Smoosic just rendering a synced, read-along staff view above it), or whether clicking/dragging directly on the staff notation becomes the way you do them, with Tonnetz-tap demoted to secondary or dropped to display-only.

It's not an all-or-nothing question either — a middle ground exists where both surfaces can edit (tap a hex *or* click a notehead, either one updates the same underlying `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]` and both views refresh), and "primary" then just means which one is documented/default/gets new features first. I left it open rather than picking, since it changes how much of Compose's existing test suite and undo-stack wiring (`[REDACTED_SECRET]`, [REDACTED_SECRET] — all currently keyed off Tonnetz-native mutators) stays as-is versus needs rethinking around a second input surface.


### 2026-08-17T02:16:08.225Z | claude (sonnet) | user | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

> How about if it becomes *a* way to do them, alongside the Tonnetz?


### 2026-08-17T02:16:42.957Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

That works, and it's cleaner than a "primary/secondary" split — both surfaces are just alternate ways to edit the same `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]`, no favorite. A few implications worth being explicit about before I write it into the doc:

1. **Sync has to become bidirectional.** A Tonnetz tap already needs to update the staff (that was true even in the "secondary view" framing). Now a staff edit also needs to update the Tonnetz lattice and push a proper undo entry — same as any other mutator.

2. **Cleanest way to get that undo entry right: route staff edits through the existing mutators, not through a second parallel edit path.** Rather than treating Smoosic's own internal document as an independent source of truth we diff against, Smoosic's edit *gestures* (click to add a note, drag a notehead to a new pitch, drag its edge to resize) should call into the same `insertAfterSelected`/`translateSelection`/etc. functions Tonnetz-tap already calls — so `js/undo-stack.js` doesn't care which surface an edit came from, and Ctrl+Z stays coherent regardless. Whether Smoosic's API actually lets us intercept edit *intent* like that (vs. only exposing "here's the new document, good luck diffing it") is unconfirmed — this becomes a load-bearing open item, not a minor one.

3. **Not every action necessarily gets a staff equivalent, and that's fine.** Translate and rotate-around-a-pivot are geometric operations specific to the hex lattice — "rotate this group 60° around a hex" has no natural staff gesture. Smoosic more realistically contributes its *own* native vocabulary (add/delete/re-pitch/re-time/select by clicking notation), while a few Tonnetz-specific operations likely stay Tonnetz-only. Worth not promising full 1:1 parity across both surfaces.

I'll update the doc to reflect this as the settled answer rather than a lean.


