1. **Strong prior on simplicity.** A kid tapping out [REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO] is producing mostly-diatonic, mostly-on-the-beat, regularly-barred material — exactly the input distribution where a naive quantizer and a simple key-fit heuristic (count pitch classes used, compare against the 24 major/minor scale profiles, pick the best match — a small, genuinely hand-rollable algorithm, not full Krumhansl-Schmuckler) work well. The failure modes that need music21-grade sophistication (heavy syncopation, chromatic passages, ambiguous meter) are rare in that population, not the common case to optimize for.

2. **The upload-your-own-MusicXML path already serves the professional case honestly.** Someone with sophisticated material has their own real notation editor already. They export MusicXML from *that* and upload it — which, per the "authored notation never needs inference" pattern already in the doc, sidesteps our quantizer/speller/barring entirely. We're not the tool asking to be as good as Finale/MuseScore for that user; we're the tool that gets out of the way and accepts their file.

