Fair question — let me explain the actual distinction, then you can decide.

**Why I split them out:** simple repeats and voltas are one well-defined [REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO] (`<repeat direction="forward/backward">` + `<ending number="1/2">`), with clean, purely local semantics — replay a measure range, pick the right ending by pass count. D.C./D.S./Coda/Fine are a different shape entirely: they're encoded as a handful of loosely-related text directives and `<sound>` attributes (`dacapo`, `dalsegno`, `tocoda`, `fine`, `segno`, `coda`), and combine in real pieces in several distinct ways — "D.C. al Fine" (go back to the start, play until the Fine mark, stop) is different logic from "D.S. al Coda" (go back to the marked Segno, play until "To Coda," then jump to the Coda section). Getting this right means recognizing that small vocabulary and building the right little navigation state machine, not one expansion pass. It's not that I think it's undesirable — it's a meaningfully bigger, differently-shaped piece of work than "expand repeats," and you'd only mentioned variant endings by name, so I didn't want to silently fold in the larger scope without flagging it.

Given that's the actual distinction (not a soft "maybe never"), do you want it in scope now alongside repeats/voltas, or left for later once the simpler expansion is working and you've seen how often real songs actually use D.C./D.S. in practice?

