That's a sharp correction, and it actually resolves cleanly rather than reopening the whole question. Let me separate the two things you just said, because they land in different places:

**Retiming stays staff-based — you're right, and it doesn't compete with the "unique value" argument at all.** The Tonnetz has no time axis. There's no gesture on a hex lattice that means "nudge this note a sixteenth note earlier" — pitch edits belong on the Tonnetz because the Tonnetz's geometry *is* pitch relationships; timing edits belong on the staff/timeline because that's the only place time is a first-class axis. These aren't competing surfaces, they're complementary ones split along exactly the axis each one actually represents. So: staff-based retiming (drag a notehead along the time axis, not vertically) stays in scope — I was wrong to lump "all staff-click editing" together and recommend dropping it wholesale.

**The select-a-segment → Tonnetz → transform → back workflow is the real center of gravity, and I think it needs less new machinery than it sounds like.** Let me trace through what you described precisely: select 3 measures (a time-range selection, so this happens on the staff/timeline, which is the surface with a time axis) → the selected notes' *pitches* project onto the Tonnetz as highlighted cells, discarding time for that view (multiple notes at the same pitch just share one highlighted hex, no extra bookkeeping needed) → drag or rotate that shape → the transform applies to every selected note's `p`/`q`, and — this is the key part — `translateSelection`/`rotateSelection` (`[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]`, already shipped) **already only ever touch `p`/`q`/`midi`, never `time`/`duration`**. That's not a coincidence I need to design around; it's already exactly the behavior you're asking for. What's actually new is narrower than "build segment-transform":

1. **Time-range selection on the staff/timeline** — click a measure, or drag across several, to select every note whose `time` falls in that range. New: today, selection only happens by tapping/shift-tapping notes directly on the Tonnetz.
2. **Confirm the Tonnetz's highlight rendering and drag-initiation already work for a selection that isn't a single contiguous tapped blob** — a 3-measure phrase likely touches several non-adjacent hexes. Needs checking, probably not a rewrite.

So the mutation logic is very likely already correct and just needs a new *entry point* into `state.selectedIndices`. That's a meaningfully smaller, sharper piece of work than "build segment transform from scratch" — and given you're saying this is the thing that proves Compose's value, not tomorrow's problem, it should be the first thing built once implementation starts, ahead of the full staff-rendering pipeline if it comes to that, since you can prototype the selection→highlight→drag→transform loop against the *existing* Tonnetz rendering without VexFlow being ready at all.

