→ Bash: git add index.html js/musicxml.js sw.js tests/desktop.spec.js tests/run_tests.js js/repeat-navigation.js git commit -m "$(cat <<'EOF' Add js/repeat-navigation.js: unroll repeats/D.C./D.S./Coda/Fine at import RepeatNavigation.resolveMeasureOrder(measureEls) -- a small, bounded navigation state machine over MusicXML's <repeat>/<ending> and the dacapo/dalsegno/tocoda/fine/segno/coda <sound> directives, producing the flat, ordered list of measure elements to actually play (repeated measures appear more than once). Wired into MusicXML.parse(), which now walks this resolved order instead of raw document order -- everything downstream of that point stays unaware repeats/jumps exist at all. Per docs/melody-notation-design.md: read-only/one-way (MusicXML.write never emits this structure, regardless of how sophisticated the reader gets) and never displayed as structure (only the unrolled note sequence is ever rendered). Deliberately bounded, not a general score-navigation engine -- exactly one repeat pass, one Segno, one Coda, which is what real folk/ children's songs (this app's actual content) need. One real bug caught via testing: Segno/Coda positions were being discovered lazily WHILE walking, but a D.S. jump physically fires from a measure that comes BEFORE the Coda section appears in the score (the whole point of jumping is reaching content not yet seen on the first pass) -- so the coda marker was still unknown exactly when a jump needed it, causing an infinite loop until the guard limit cut it off. Fixed with a pre-scan for both markers before the navigation walk runs at all. Tests cover: a simple repeat (twice through), first/second endings, D.C. al Fine, D.S. al Coda, and confirming the whole mechanism is a no-op for MusicXML.write's own output (which never contains this structure). Co-Authored-By: [REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO] <[REDACTED_EMAIL]> EOF )"


