### 2026-08-17T21:00:56.865Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

You're right, and I was wrong — retracting the Handoff theory. I checked git history and found the actual explanation:

`playwright.config.js`'s "Mobile Chrome" project used `devices['iPhone 13']` (Chromium-based) until commit `[REDACTED_SECRET]` on [REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO] changed it to `devices['Pixel 5']` (Android) to make room for a separate iOS/Safari profile. That's about three weeks before now.

`tests/invariants.spec.js`'s `[REDACTED_STUDENT]` test explicitly does `context.grantPermissions(['clipboard-read', 'clipboard-write'])` and then exercises the real "copy to clipboard" path (`navigator.clipboard.writeText`) — and Playwright's browser, even under test automation, is a genuine Chromium process running on your actual Mac. With that permission granted, the write goes to the *real* OS clipboard, not a sandboxed one.

So: at some point before [REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO], a test run of `[REDACTED_STUDENT]` under the old iPhone-emulating "Mobile Chrome" config legitimately overwrote your system clipboard with synthetic fuzz-test replay data (spoofed iPhone user-agent, scattered clicks — matches exactly what you pasted, including `maxTouchPoints: 0`, which is a giveaway of emulation rather than a real device). It's been sitting there undisturbed for roughly three weeks until you pasted it to me.

This is a real testing-hygiene issue worth naming: running the local test suite has a side effect of clobbering your real system clipboard. Want me to fix `[REDACTED_STUDENT]` to stub `navigator.clipboard.writeText` instead of granting a real permission, so running tests locally can't do this again?


