### 2026-08-19T01:04:55.278Z | claude (sonnet) | user | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

> So I disagree with the way you said it though. Why would the visual position o the end marker be to the left of what is the actual end?


### 2026-08-19T01:05:28.839Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

You're right, and my comment overstated it. Let me walk through why.

The Timeline's `_positionMarker` draws each marker at the literal x-position of whichever note index it's set to (`entry.x`, from `noteXPositions`) — it snaps onto notes, never onto a boundary between them. That's an implementation choice, not a necessity. If the end marker were instead drawn at a boundary (the gap *after* the last included note, like a text caret sitting between characters rather than on one), an exclusive `[0, 1)` — one note, note 0 only — could legitimately place the end marker visually to the *right* of the start marker, distinct from it, marking "everything up to here." Nothing about exclusive semantics forces the two markers to coincide.

So "defeating the point of the fix" was wrong — that's only true *given* the marker-sits-on-a-note rendering we happen to have. The real, narrower reason `[0, 1]` was chosen is just: under our current on-the-note marker convention, two distinct visual positions require two distinct note indices, and inclusive-inclusive is what the rest of the code (`startIndex`/`endIndex` as real note indices, symmetric with each other) already assumes throughout. Let me fix the comment to say that accurately instead of the overreaching claim.


