Yes, exactly that. VexFlow's `getAbsoluteX()` returns a note's bounding-box left edge (its stem/tick anchor), not the visual center of the notehead glyph. Because there's real spacing between adjacent notes, that left-edge x lands in the gap after the previous note and before this note's own glyph — so a thin marker drawn there reads visually as sitting *between* the two, even though the underlying computation is "this note's own x." Same mechanism, just anchored to the start of the glyph rather than its middle.


