### 2026-08-19T01:18:47.928Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

I looked at this closely, and there's a real gap: `[REDACTED_SECRET]` only records `keydown`/`pointerdown`/`pointerup`/`resize`/`orientationchange`/`visibilitychange` as discrete `events`. It has no listener on MIDI input at all — a MIDI-driven session's note-presses never become a recorded *event*, only a *sound* (`Replay.wrapSynth()` logs every `Synth.playNote()` call into `sounds`, regardless of whether it came from the app's own auto-play of the target sequence or from you actually pressing a key).

That's exactly what happened here: your `events` array has only 3 incidental clicks (the source dropdown, the MIDI connect button, the bug-report link), and all ~110 notes live only in `sounds` — which mixes "the app played this as the target sequence" and "you played this on your keyboard" indistinguishably, since both go through the same `Synth.playNote` call. There's no way to tell them apart from this data, and I'm not going to fabricate a plausible-looking input sequence to fill the gap — `[REDACTED_SECRET]`'s own header specifically calls out a past version of this file that did something like that ("wildly unfaithful") as a real mistake to not repeat.

So this particular file can't become a faithful story test as-is. Two ways forward:


