### 2026-08-19T01:20:38.888Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

You're right, and the honest answer is: no test caught this because **no test exists** for it. I searched — nothing in the suite asserts "Compose defaults to Record your own" at all, let alone across a Melody→Compose switch. This isn't a false-positive test; it's a real, untested gap.

Digging into why: `[REDACTED_SECRET]` explicitly makes `MidiFolder` **one shared object instance** that Melody and Compose both call `.setup()` on — its own comment says so: "Melody and Compose call `MidiFolder.setup()` with their own DOM ids but this SAME instance, so they browse (and remember) the one folder of songs together." That was a deliberate decision from earlier in this session, in service of a "browse together" feature. But it directly contradicts [REDACTED_SECRET]'s "No shared mutable state between modes... must never be the same object another mode reads or writes" — nobody reconciled the two at the time, and I should have caught that tension when I made it.

The real fix is to stop sharing the object. The one part that's legitimate to share is *which folder is connected* — `FileFolder`'s IndexedDB persistence (`DB_NAME`/`STORE_NAME`/`HANDLE_KEY`) is already declared at the shared-namespace level, not per-instance, so two independent instances would still both read/write the same remembered folder automatically. What shouldn't be shared is `currentValue`/`fileHandles` — which file is currently *selected* is exactly the kind of per-mode UI state [REDACTED_SECRET] is about.


