### 2026-08-19T03:48:03.265Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

Your read is right, and it's sharper than what I actually reasoned through — let me confirm the shape of it.

With full-height markers, "[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]" works whenever there's somewhere else to drag it *to*. The invariant (`[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]`) is exactly what removes that room at the edges[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO] Concretely — a single-note piece has `start == end == 0` always; there's no "away" for either marker to go that doesn't collapse the range. A two-note piece is only slightly better: you can separate them, but one of them is always still parked on note 0 or note 1.

That's a real, structural dead end, not just inconvenience — no sequence of drags gets you there, so it'd need a *third* interaction (some way to temporarily disarm a marker, or a modifier-click that reaches through it) to fully recover, which is more machinery than the height-shrink costs.

The other cost I'd weigh against your version: even off the edges, "click does nothing until you first drag this unrelated thing out of the way" is a discoverability tax on the single most basic Compose action (click to add/select a note) — paid on every session, not just the edge case. The height-shrink makes note-editing unconditional; the drag-away version makes it usually-fine-but-occasionally-you-need-to-know-a-trick.

Given that, I'd keep what's there. But if the visual mismatch with Melody bothers you more than I'm weighing it, the compromise might be: full height as the *default* hit target, but with a `pointer-events: none` "notch" cut out wherever a real note's own x-position falls under the marker's current span — lets the marker still look tall/prominent everywhere else, while never actually blocking a note click. That's more CSS/JS than the current one-line height override though, and I'd only build it if the current tiny-marker look is actually bothering you day to day.


