### 2026-08-19T04:52:19.586Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

No mode-based hiding — the buttons stay visible everywhere, they just silently no-op when the current mode lacks the method. I have enough now to answer.

## Copy/paste in Melody vs Compose vs Sandbox

You're right on both counts.

**Melody implements neither.** `MelodyMode` has no `copyCells` or `pasteClipboard` (`[REDACTED_SECRET]` both guard on `typeof m.copyCells/pasteClipboard !== 'function'` and silently no-op). So the header Copy/Paste buttons and Ctrl+C/V do nothing while you're in Melody — not "copies nothing," but literally doesn't run. And because `App.clipboard` is one global array, not per-mode, switching into Melody doesn't clear or touch it — whatever you last copied from Compose or Sandbox just sits there inert until you switch to a mode that can paste it.

**Compose** (`[REDACTED_SECRET]`):
- *Copy* = every note's `(p,q)` cell only — no time, no duration, no pitch-octave-as-played. It's the piece's harmonic shape, stripped of rhythm.
- *Paste* = drop all those cells in as one simultaneous chord at the playhead (end of the current piece), each getting `DEFAULT_DURATION`. So pasting a whole melody back into Compose collapses it into a single chord, not a re-sequenced phrase — rhythm doesn't round-trip.

**Sandbox** (`[REDACTED_SECRET]`, same shape):
- *Copy* = every placed piece's cells + any individually-pasted cells, again just `(p,q)`.
- *Paste* = add each cell to `placedCells` (individual hexes, not reconstituted carousel pieces) and redraw. No time dimension there anyway, so nothing is lost that Sandbox could have kept.

So concretely: **paste from a Melody copy** never happens (Melody can't copy). **Paste from a Compose copy** — into Compose, becomes one chord at the end; into Sandbox, becomes loose placed hexes. **Paste from a Sandbox copy** — same two destinations, same two behaviors, since both consumers only care about `(p,q)` and neither knows anything about where the cells originally came from.

