### 2026-08-19T19:44:24.641Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

Found it, and confirmed it live. Two separate answers:

**Why the story test didn't catch it:** Gravity doesn't have a story test at all — only Blast does (`[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]` has exactly one test). And even that one doesn't clear a single line in its own recorded session — its own header comment notes that coverage was lost when an earlier viewBox-fit change shifted where the recorded clicks land, so the replayed session now ends before completing a row. So there was never a story test anywhere near this code path.

**The actual bug**, reproduced directly: `settleFloatingCellsStep` recomputes connected components from scratch every tick, and any single cell of a falling mass touching *any* existing pile cell — even one unrelated leftover fragment nowhere near where most of the mass should land — instantly freezes the *entire* rigid mass as "resting." I built this exact scenario: a wide 9-cell mass above a row that clears, sharing the board with an unrelated single-column spike elsewhere. It fell one row cleanly, but the moment its edge brushed past the spike's tip, the whole mass locked in place — 8 of its 9 cells stuck hovering in open air for the rest of the run, even though nothing was actually under them. In any real, jagged pile from actual play, debris sliding down is very likely to brush *something* on the way past, so this reads as "stuff doesn't fall" almost immediately after a clear — matching what you saw.

This is the same rigid-shape-preservation model that's correct for an *active falling piece* (it should rest as one shape, not shear) but wrong for post-clear debris, where real Tetris-style physics wants each column settling independently, not one contact point locking the whole mass.

Given you'd already said pasted cells should behave as independent, decoupled 1x1s — do you want that same independence applied to all post-clear debris now too (each cell/column falls on its own, no rigid grouping for anything except the active piece), or something narrower?


### 2026-08-19T20:59:12.915Z | claude (sonnet) | user | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

> Actually slightly narrower: some pieces overhang because they were connected when they fell, and those should remain a rigid piece. So if a cell that's already down is overhanging, it should rigidly connect to the closest piece that had it do so in the first place, perhaps transitively. A line clear shouldn't make already-settled pieces above it change their relative relationship to each other. But they should be disconnected from anything off-board that cannot be cleared, or else we get this mess. I *think* that just means disconnecting from the off-board cells which might mean cutting and clearing the off-board cells as soon as a shape settles and it becomes clear which cells are no longer in the cup. I bet the rest behaves as it should without those.


