### 2026-08-19T21:12:45.153Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

Summary while the full suite runs in the background: I implemented both pieces of your fix.

**Origin-based rigid groups** — `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]` entries now carry a `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]`, stamped once at lock time (`[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]`, all cells of one piece share an id) or paste time (`[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]`, each pasted cell gets its own id — matching "individual cells"). `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]` now just groups by that stored id instead of recomputing adjacency via BFS every tick, so two originally-separate pieces that happen to touch stay independently falling — one is blocked by ordinary occupancy collision against the other, but they never fuse into one rigid mass.

**Off-board trimming** — you were right that this is a real, related bug: `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]` already lets a piece lock while overhanging the side wall (a "toe-hold" is enough), and those off-grid cells used to sit in `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]` forever (`[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]` never scans past col 4). `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]` now deletes them at lock time instead of leaving them as permanent, uncleaable clutter.

One correction to your bet, though — I tested it directly: off-board trimming *alone* doesn't fix the freeze. My repro (a single-column spike, fully in-bounds, real settled terrain from ordinary play) still froze a falling mass the instant it brushed past it, with no wall-overhang involved at all. Both fixes are needed together; I verified each one is independently red on the pre-fix code and green after, via two new tests.

Waiting on the full 246-test suite now before committing.


