### 2026-08-20T02:45:21.233Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

I found the exact lock. Traced precisely: it's `L`-piece lock #22 — you pressed ArrowLeft three times right before it hit bottom (all three registered fine, no dropped input), and it still locked at `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]` with cells `[(2,2), (3,1), (4,0), (5,0)]`. That last cell, `(5,0)`, is at column 5 — one past the cup's right wall (`-5..4`). Under `checkActivePlacement`'s long-standing "toe-hold" rule (predates all my recent changes), a piece is allowed to overhang the wall while steering as long as *some* cell has a toe-hold in bounds; if it locks while still overhanging, the overhanging cell is lost.

So this isn't the connectivity bug — I checked: `(2,2)` and `(3,1)` (the two survivors after the later full-row clear also removed `(4,0)`) are still genuinely adjacent to each other, no fusion, no freeze. This is the wall trim working exactly as designed, just visibly losing a cell of your piece because it was still one column short of fully clearing the wall when it locked.

Two honest possibilities, and I'd rather ask than guess:

1. **This is old, pre-existing behavior** you're only now noticing because my off-board-trim fix (from your first report) made the loss *immediate* — before that fix, the overhanging cell used to sit there forever as invisible, uncleared clutter instead of vanishing, so it may not have visibly read as "a gap" before.
2. Or you're saying the wall-overhang-loses-a-cell rule itself is wrong, full stop — a piece shouldn't ever be allowed to lock while any part of it hangs outside the cup; it should either be blocked from locking there, or bounced/nudged fully inside first.

Which of those matches what you meant?


