Found the exact bug — and it's exactly what you described. Confirmed via direct trace: `settleFloatingCellsStep` picks its "down" direction from `comp[0]` — *whichever* cell happens to be first in `GravityBoard.cells`'s Map iteration order — not from a stable reference tied to the piece itself. For your fragment `[REDACTED_PERSON]`+`[REDACTED_PERSON]`, one step picked `[REDACTED_PERSON]` as reference (even q → offset `(+1,-1)`), shifting *both* cells diagonally right-and-down instead of straight down, landing the pair one column further right than a consistent fall would have produced. The active piece avoids this exact problem by always using its own fixed `state.p/q` anchor — settled groups had no equivalent, so their "down" choice could silently flip between ticks based on incidental Map reordering.

Fixing it by giving every group a stable anchor cell, assigned once at group-creation time, that travels with it through moves (since the flag lives on the cell's value object, which is the same object reference relocated on each move — no separate bookkeeping needed).


