→ Bash: git -C [REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO] commit -m "$(cat <<'EOF' Gravity: settled groups fall using their piece's true anchor, not comp[0] Reported live, pinpointed precisely by the player: after a piece locked and completed a line, its uncleared cells landed one column to the right of where they should have -- and it couldn't be the wall-overhang trim (a trimmed piece can't complete a line), so it had to be something about how the survivors fell afterward. Traced directly against the real replay (js/replay.js + scripts/replay-to- gif.js): settleFloatingCellsStep picked its "down" direction from comp[0] -- whichever cell happened to be first in the group's own array, an accident of Pieces.TYPES' own cell-list order -- not the piece's TRUE anchor (state.p, state.q). The hex grid has two valid "straight down" offsets depending on q's parity; a rigid group's members can have different parities, so anchoring on the wrong cell picks the wrong offset and drags the whole shape a column off from where the piece's own anchor (matching exactly how it fell before it ever locked) would have carried it. Fixed by giving every group a persistent isAnchor flag, set once at group-creation time and carried forward on the cell's own value object (the same object relocated on every move, so no separate bookkeeping is needed). lockActivePiece passes the piece's true (state.p, state.q) as the preferred anchor; _resplitGroups passes through whichever surviving cell already held it. Either falls back to a deterministic comp[0] when the true anchor itself was trimmed or cleared -- there's no correct anchor to prefer once it's gone. Verified against the real replay (the fragment now settles straight down to ['2,1','3,0'] instead of drifting to ['3,1','4,0']) and two new tests, both confirmed red on the pre-fix code first. Co-Authored-By: [REDACTED_PERSON] EOF )" git -C [REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO] log --oneline -1


