### 2026-08-20T07:34:26.600Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

All three, precisely traced:

**15→16 and 31→32 (the block-of-6/block-of-3 splits, and the left gap)**: Both are the same phenomenon, and it's exactly what "piece-origin rigidity" is designed to do. What looks like one contiguous block of 6 is actually *multiple separate pieces* that happen to be touching — each keeps falling according to its own anchor and its own independent left/right slide choice when blocked, not as one welded mass. That's deliberate: it's the fix from a few sessions ago (pieces that merely touch stay independent; only cells that fell together as one piece stay rigid together). The alternative — welding touching pieces into one group once they come to rest — would bring back the freeze bug we spent real effort fixing, since a big welded mass can get permanently stuck the instant any one part of it touches something. So this is a real tradeoff, not an oversight. If you want, I can look at a narrower version — e.g., only weld pieces together at the moment they *first come to rest* touching each other (not while both are still independently falling) — but that's a design change worth deciding on deliberately, not something to just do.

**28→29**: This one's a genuinely different mechanism, and I'm glad you flagged it — it wasn't shape-matching at all. The locked piece was the `I`-piece lying along the diagonal axis (`[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]-(-1,3)-(-2,4)-(-3,5)`), and it contributed exactly **one** cell, `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]`, which happened to be the single missing cell completing row q=2. That one clear let everything above resettle — which completed row q=1 on the very next tick, which triggered another resettle that completed row q=0 the tick after. Three full-line clears, cascading, from one 4-cell placement — only the first was the piece's own doing; the other two were emergent consequences of debris falling into place afterward. The "straight piece filling a crooked fissure" you saw was mostly that cascade, not the piece's own shape conforming to anything crooked.


### 2026-08-20T07:42:12.034Z | claude (sonnet) | user | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

> Right. Like I said, if I think hard about the physics of it, I get it. Consider a possibility for the "happen to be touching" scenario: something like static electricity. If you happen to be touching another piece, and you've got two valid choices of where to fall and in one of them you keep touching the other piece, choose that? So it's not like it's a hard thing, but just a choice. You could even imagine this being difficulty-controlled. Like sure, on easy we'll stay together. On harder we'll do whatever we want and make confusing fissures. It's kind of mind-bendingly fun if you let it be. So I'd like to think about that as a possible trade-off. You made another good suggestion: weld pieces when they first come to rest touching each other, not while they're independently falling -- that makes sense too. I'm aware that [REDACTED_SECRET] is not a bug, and it could be a genuine feature that separates this tris from rectangular trises and makes it extra fun. That's why I was careful to say that I was surprised, not that it was necessarily a problem.


### 2026-08-20T07:42:52.528Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

That framing is a good one — and honestly, "weld at rest" turns out to be the safer building block for both ideas, not just an alternative to static-cling: since a group only ever gets fragmented correctly via `[REDACTED_SECRET]`'s connectivity check (already proven safe by the freeze fix), welding two genuinely-touching, already-resting groups into one is symmetric and low-risk — a later clear cutting through the merged mass still resplits it correctly, same as any other piece. Static cling is the more interesting idea but a deeper change: right now there's no real "choice" node in the code, just one primary offset and one blocked-fallback offset picked deterministically by parity — giving debris an actual preference between two open paths means generalizing that fallback to evaluate both directions when both are open, which is more surgery on the core fall logic.

My recommendation: build "weld at rest" first — it's the smaller, well-scoped piece, reuses infrastructure you already trust, and is naturally difficulty-gateable (easy welds, hard never does) the same way Blast/Gravity's piece-size difficulty already works. Static cling is worth keeping on the list as a v2 once weld-at-rest's difficulty-tunable framing is in place, since "prefer contact" is really the *soft* version of the same knob.

Not proposing we implement either right now — just flagging that's where I'd start if/when you want to move on this.


