### 2026-08-20T08:29:04.038Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

Good instinct, and the numbers back it up hard: across this session, **[REDACTED_SECRET]** That's genuinely pervasive, not an edge case.

But I want to push back gently on the mechanism, because it changes what the right fix is. Off-board cells were never able to "come back in" — even *before* any of my changes, falling straight down never changes a cell's column (that's a real invariant of the hex grid math, not something I introduced). So the trim didn't make that material *newly* inaccessible; it was always structurally stuck. What the trim actually changed is only *visibility*: before, an overhanging piece sat there forever, visible, poking past the wall — the charm you're describing. My fix made it vanish immediately instead.

That distinction matters because it means I don't think you need the big revert. The freeze-bug fixes (connectivity-safe grouping, the anchor fix) are correctness fixes for a real, severe regression you hit independently of any of this — reverting those would bring that back. But the *trim itself* was only ever a safety measure against the old bug where a stray off-board fragment could incorrectly weld to and freeze unrelated debris. That bug is now fixed at the root (`_assignGroupId`'s own connectivity split), so the trim's original justification is gone. An untrimmed overhang piece just becomes permanent, inert furniture — exactly the original, long-standing, "charming" behavior — and anything that later welds onto it (difficulty ≤3) becoming fixed too seems like a reasonable, thematically consistent consequence, not a bug.

So my recommendation: **revert just the off-board trim**, restore the original "hangs there forever, visible" behavior, keep everything else. Want me to do that?


