### 2026-08-20T08:33:37.209Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

You're right, and I see the error now — I proved column-preservation only for the *straight-down* option. The diagonal slide (the "left-right choice" you're describing) genuinely shifts column by one, and that's exactly how an overhanging piece could walk itself back into bounds over several ticks. I missed that entirely.

But there's a deeper mismatch causing this: the **active piece** (while steering) uses a lenient "toe-hold" rule — only one cell needs to be in-bounds, the rest can overhang. But **settled debris** (`[REDACTED_SECRET]`'s `[REDACTED_SECRET]`) requires *every* cell in-bounds to move at all. So even without my trim, an overhanging piece would go rigid the instant it locks — it can never take that recovery slide, because the rule governing its movement changed the moment it stopped being "active." That's the real bug: trimming just made the symptom immediate instead of leaving it stuck-but-invisible forever.

Let me fix both pieces — stop deleting the overhang, and make `[REDACTED_SECRET]` use the same toe-hold rule as the active piece — and verify against your replay before claiming anything.


