### 2026-08-20T09:06:31.123Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

Option 1 is the better first move — it directly targets what's actually slow (the up-to-one-tick delay between a line visually completing and it clearing), costs nothing architecturally (just call the clear-check immediately at the two places cells get added — `lockActivePiece` and after `settleFloatingCellsStep`'s moves — instead of only once per scheduled tick), and doesn't touch settle speed at all, which you said is fine as-is.

Option 2 solves a different problem (cascades taking many real ticks to resolve) and costs a lot more: a pause/resume state machine, a temporary speed multiplier, and care around not making player input feel weird while it's in fast-forward. I'd hold off on it until you've tried Option 1 and can tell whether the *cascade* itself (not just the initial clear trigger) still feels sluggish.

