### 2026-08-22T18:02:28.191Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

The fix lives entirely in `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]` (the offline reconstruction tool) — nothing in `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]` changes.

After processing each recorded event, if `SnakeMode.state.isFlourishing` is `true`, the tool explicitly advances the frozen page clock by the same duration `playFlourish()` itself would take to finish (`snake.length * 100 + 250` ms, recomputed live from the actual snake length at that moment) via `page.clock.runFor(duration)`. That lets all of that flourish's real timeouts — the per-cell note timeouts and the finish timeout — actually fire in order, exactly as they would in real play, before the tool moves on to the next event. The finish timeout flips `isFlourishing` back to `false` and calls `startTimer()` again, so `tick()` stops early-returning and movement resumes.

One extra step on top of that: `startTimer()` restarting creates a new live `setInterval`. Since the tool drives movement by calling `tick()` directly rather than letting any interval fire, I clear that new interval immediately after each flourish resolves — otherwise it sits dormant and could fire an extra, unaccounted-for tick during some *later* flourish's `runFor()` call, desyncing the reconstruction from the recorded tick count.

Separately (a distinct but related gap, not the freeze itself): the tool's frame-capture heuristic only snapshots on a change in `Board.cells.size`/`SandboxMode`'s piece count, which Snake has neither of, so it was falling back to capturing once every 3 real recorded seconds — sparse enough that even non-frozen movement would look like disconnected snapshots rather than a smooth step-by-step replay. I'd also switch that to fire on every change in the snake's actual body positions, so consecutive frames show single-step movement.


