### 2026-08-24T05:48:52.004Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

Yes — **[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]**, per this project's own categorization. The deciding factor isn't screen resolution, it's touch capability: the app itself gates touch-specific behavior on `'ontouchstart' in window || navigator.maxTouchPoints > 0` (`[REDACTED_SECRET]`), and the Playwright projects mirror that exactly — Mobile Chrome/Tablet Chrome both set `hasTouch: true`, [REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO] Chrome doesn't. A no-touch device never exercises those code paths regardless of how low-res it is.

One nuance worth flagging: "[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]" here only means *no-touch*, not *never sees the responsive mobile layout*. The CSS still has width-based breakpoints for narrow viewports independent of touch, so a low-res no-touch Chromebook could still land in the app's mobile/tablet *visual* layout while behaving as "[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]" in the touch-interaction sense. If you ever want a story specifically for that device, it'd belong in `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]` ([REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO] project) with a narrow `setViewportSize`, not in `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]` — same as how Blast's existing story already runs at a narrow 1179×868 desktop window today.


