You're right — I traced through it. Portrait unwraps both clusters into one shared row via `[REDACTED_SECRET]` and uses the same DOM order landscape's columns use. My landscape fix (reordering DOM to `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]`) fixed the column but broke portrait's `◀ ↺ ▼ ↻ ▶` mirror-symmetric row into `◀ ↺ ▼ ▶ ↻`. A single DOM order can't satisfy both layouts at once — portrait wants `cw` before `right`, landscape wants `right` before `cw`. Fixing this properly means reverting the DOM order and using CSS `order` (landscape-only) to control column position independently of portrait's row order.


