Checked the data directly rather than assuming. The full game (52 steering presses across all 393 ticks) is densely and evenly recorded — gaps mostly 1-15 ticks, matching normal play. It is **not** "nearly all taps lost." The gap is isolated to the ~11 ticks between mode-switch and the first recorded press.

I traced the code path for that specific window and ruled out two things:

- **Drawer overlap** — `[REDACTED_SECRET]` isn't inside `[REDACTED_SECRET]` at all (checked `[REDACTED_SECRET]`); it's a sibling under `<aside>`. And `[REDACTED_SECRET]` calls `[REDACTED_SECRET]` immediately on mode selection anyway. Not the cause.
- **A binding race** — `[REDACTED_SECRET]` (which starts the reset/timer) and `[REDACTED_SECRET]` (which makes the D-pad `display:flex` and binds the buttons) both run synchronously in the same call stack inside `[REDACTED_SECRET]`, with no `await`/`setTimeout` between them. By the time the mode-tab click handler returns, the D-pad is already visible and bound. No JS-level race window exists there.

What I can't explain: the recorded log shows *zero* events at all in that gap — not even a misdirected tap landing on the wrong element (which the window-level `pointerdown` listener would still have caught, just with a different `target`). That points to something swallowing the touch before it ever becomes a `pointerdown` — real Android Chrome's touch-vs-scroll gesture disambiguation is the remaining plausible suspect (a fresh touch near a just-shown element can get held pending scroll-intent resolution), but I can't verify that without a real device.

Bottom line: this needs a fresh capture focused on exactly that moment, not more static analysis of the existing file. If you can reproduce it — switch into Snake, steer within the first second, then grab the replay via 🦟 — I can pinpoint it directly instead of guessing further.


