### 2026-08-27T23:17:06.432Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

Here's the breakdown. First, the important baseline finding: the drawer's own content (title/icons + mode-tabs) is **identical across every mode** — nothing mode-specific lives inside `#top-drawer` itself. `#drawer-injected-tools` exists in the markup but is currently dead code (always hidden, never populated). The only mode-specific content anywhere in the header cluster is Sandbox's, and it lives in `#mobile-always-visible` — the always-shown strip between the drawer and the game board, not the drawer itself.

| Mode | In the drawer (mode-specific) | In the always-visible header strip (mode-specific) | In main content (mode-specific) |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Sandbox** | — (none) | Chord-guide dropdown + reset button + results (moved here from `#sandbox-guide` on mobile only) | `#palette` (piece carousel), `#sandbox-guide` (chord instructions) — desktop: both in `#sidebar`; mobile: palette moves into the always-visible strip too |
| **Melody** | — (none) | — (none) | `#melody-controls` + its notation-scroll — moved into `#notation-bar` (a bar over the game area) at *every* viewport, not just mobile |
| **Compose** | — (none) | — (none) | `#compose-controls` + its notation-scroll — same `#notation-bar` treatment as Melody |
| **Snake** | — (none) | — (none) | `#snake-controls` in `#sidebar`; mobile adds `#snake-mobile-controls` (floating D-pad over the board) |
| **Blast** | — (none) | — (none) | `#blast-stats` in `#sidebar`; `#palette` doubles as the next-piece queue (floats over the board on mobile); mobile adds `#mobile-controls` (shared D-pad) |
| **Gravity** | — (none) | — (none) | `#gravity-controls` in `#sidebar`; `#palette` as floating next-piece queue on mobile; mobile adds `#mobile-controls` (same shared D-pad as Blast) |
| **Life** | — (none) | — (none) | `#life-controls` in `#sidebar` |

The practical implication for the redesign: since the drawer itself carries no per-mode content, there's no risk of the new collapsed "thin rail" needing to show mode-specific *tools* — only the mode-*indicator* (icon/label) discussed earlier. The one wrinkle is Sandbox's chord-tools sitting in that always-visible strip between drawer and board — worth deciding whether that strip also needs edge-anchoring treatment, or whether it's fine staying where it is since it's genuinely always visible (not something the collapse/expand state affects).


