### 2026-08-28T14:46:48.256Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

Found and fixed three real bugs across your four reports:

1. **`[REDACTED_HIGH_ENTROPY_TOKEN]`** (and the **`[REDACTED_HIGH_ENTROPY_TOKEN]`** one too — same bug, any mode): `#top-drawer` still had its base 20px-each-side horizontal padding even when collapsed. Portrait's collapse (height:0) hides that padding for free; landscape collapses via `max-width:0` instead, so the padding rendered as real visible width on a "collapsed" drawer. Wrote a regression test for it (`[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]`), confirmed it failed, fixed it, confirmed green.

2. **`[REDACTED_HIGH_ENTROPY_TOKEN]`** (and the other two you mentioned): the new floating panel was quietly inheriting `height: 100%` from the base desktop `#sidebar` rule, which — combined with the `max-height: 200px` cap — made it always render at exactly 200px regardless of how little content it had. Fixed with an explicit `height: auto` so it shrinks to fit.

3. **`[REDACTED_HIGH_ENTROPY_TOKEN]`**: capped the panel at `max-width: 55%` instead of full width, still anchored to the left edge per your earlier "should start flush left" note.

All committed, with the existing Sandbox/board-interaction tests re-verified green. Want me to kick off another full suite run to refresh `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]` with these fixes, or are you good spot-checking a few once you're ready?


