### 2026-08-29T17:51:12.567Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

Good catch — you were right to push back. Root cause is different from what I first reported:

**It's not occupation, it's out-of-bounds coordinates being misreported as "occupied."** `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]` is defined as `isInBounds(p,q) && !cells.has(...)` — so a cell that's simply *outside* Gravity's narrow 10-column cup shape reports as "not empty," identically to a cell that's genuinely occupied by existing pile content. There's no way to tell the two apart from the paste code's perspective.

I verified this directly: copying 3 genuinely-empty cells from [REDACTED_STUDENT] (nothing placed there) at `(10,10)`, `(11,10)`, `(10,11)`, then pasting into a **completely empty** Gravity board — nothing gets placed. Every copied cell converts (via `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]`) to a position like `(-27, 14)`, far outside the cup's actual bounds, so `isInBounds` is false for all three, and the paste silently rejects the entire clipboard.

This matches your experience exactly: you correctly picked empty cells in the source mode, but Gravity's playable area is a small, specific hexagonal cup — most of the coordinate space that looks like open room in an unrestricted mode ([REDACTED_STUDENT], or Gravity's own view before/after the cup) simply isn't part of the cup at all, and pasting there fails with no indication that "out of bounds" is the actual reason, not "occupied."

Want this fixed now (e.g., distinguish out-of-bounds from occupied and give real feedback), or logged as a proper bug for later?


