### 2026-08-30T03:03:20.060Z | claude (sonnet) | claude tools | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

→ Bash: gh issue comment 30 --repo [REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO] --body "Both reported problems fixed and tested: **Level 4 (\"Chaos\") removed entirely** — \`[REDACTED_SECRET]\`. The extra difficulty level and its no-welding physics variant are gone; welding at rest now always applies. Difficulty barbell is back to 3 levels. **Paste failure root-caused and fixed** — the real story took a wrong turn first: - \`[REDACTED_SECRET]\` — an initial fix (checking only the literal + immediate ±1-step neighbor) was wrong and reverted in \`[REDACTED_SECRET]\`: it couldn't find an in-bounds cell more than one step away, which is a real case here. - \`[REDACTED_SECRET]\` — the correct fix. Root cause: \`Tonnetz.canonicalToGravity\`'s coordinate conversion is only ONE of up to two possible embeddings of a given pitch onto the gravity lattice (the two differ by exactly the one lattice vector that preserves pitch), and the cup's narrow bounds mean a pitch never has more than 2 in-bounds cells — but the literal conversion can land arbitrarily far out of bounds along that pitch's infinite line. Paste now scores each whole-clipboard shift (applied identically to every cell) by how many cells it seats, and uses whichever seats the most — keeping a connected multi-cell shape together rather than resolving each cell's fallback independently, which could scatter a shape's cells across two different lattice copies of the same pattern. - Covered by two new tests in \`tests/desktop.spec.js\`: a single out-of-bounds cell falling back to its pitch-preserving alternate, and a connected two-cell shape staying connected under a shared shift. Closing — verified via full suite (585/586, the one failure unrelated and separately fixed in \`d374bff\`)."


