### 2026-08-30T12:09:57.787Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

Reviewed all 10 commits Codex made overnight (`[REDACTED_SECRET]`, ~23:06–23:24 last night — Melody chord/partial-credit support, adaptive mistake recovery, per-note performance coloring, grouped notation, MIDI wake-lock, Compose polyphonic import). Ran the new test suite (`[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]`, 11/11 green) and read the diffs closely rather than trusting green tests alone. Found two real issues the tests don't catch:

**1. Bug: a wrong note mid-chord flashes an already-correct chord member red too.** In `handleUserInputNote`'s mistake branch (`[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]`), `flashMistakeNotes(eventIndices)` is called with *every* index in the current chord event, not just the unmatched ones — unlike the line right above it, which correctly filters to `!matched.includes(index)` before recording misses. I verified this live: play the correct first note of a 2-note chord (accepted, no flash), then a wrong note for the second — the *already-correct* first note flashes red too, contradicting its own accumulated green/correct state. One-line fix: filter `eventIndices` to unmatched before passing to `flashMistakeNotes`, same as the `recordNotePerformance` call right above it. I left a scratch repro at `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]` per your call, so Codex has a failing test to fix against.

**2. Bug: staff notehead coloring silently drops per-member feedback when a chord shares a clef.** `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]`'s own added invariant says *"Each member's visual state reflects its own performance result."* True for the pitch-row labels (`decorate`, keyed per-index) — but in `notation.js`, the grouped-chord render only applies `decorateNote(members[0])` (`[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]`) to the whole shared VexFlow notehead, so if two chord members land in the same clef (the common case — any close-voiced chord within an octave near middle C) and have different performance states (one correct, one missed), the staff notehead shows only the first member's color. Only the pitch row below stays accurate. The new test ("Melody exposes per-note success and mistake states in the pitch row") only checks the pitch row, so this gap is untested and passes clean.

**3. Minor/lower-confidence:** the chord-grouping logic now lives in two independent places — `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]`'s `getEventBounds` groups by raw time within ±0.08s of the first note, while `notation.js`'s renderer groups by quantized `beatStart` equality. They'll usually agree on real quantized MIDI data, but they're not the same test, so it's plausible (not verified) some import could have the matcher and the renderer disagree about which notes are "one chord."

**4. Housekeeping:** the new invariant test is titled `INV-23` (`[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]`), but INV-23 is already a real, documented invariant (live MIDI input ≡ tap) with its own section in `docs/invariants.md`. The wake-lock test needs its own number (next free is INV-57) and a `docs/invariants.md` entry — right now it's undocumented.

Everything else — chord partial-credit advancement logic, the Random-mode "no fake win screen" fix, the difficulty-window fix for issue #31, MIDI wake-lock request/reacquire lifecycle, `extractMonophonicMelody` retirement from the live import path — looks sound and matches what it claims to do.

Want me to fix #1 and #4 now (both quick, low-risk), or leave the whole batch for Codex since it's mid-flight on this feature?


