### 2026-08-15T00:15:13Z | agy | agy | conversation 4abc6e66-08cd-4cb4-9377-f2da45e943a9

So, the symmetric search finished and produced a few hits, but when I stepped through them with a debugger, I discovered another deep structural truth about `[REDACTED_SECRET]`.

The script thought it found a 3-fold rotationally symmetric gun because at generation 25, the 64-cell seed exploded into 22 chaotic components, including **3 gliders shooting outward symmetrically**. 
But by generation 60, the explosion died down, leaving behind a perfectly stable, static core of 18 cells, and the 3 gliders just kept flying away forever. It never shot a second wave.

This confirms what we saw earlier: `[REDACTED_SECRET]` easily synthesizes gliders from random debris, but it strongly resists *periodic* glider emission. Cores tend to either explode completely or freeze into simple period-2/period-3 oscillators that refuse to interact further.

To get around this, we need to cheat. We need a **[REDACTED_SECRET]**.
A puffer is a spaceship that translates across the board, but unlike a clean glider, it leaves a trail of debris behind it. If we can find a bilaterally symmetric [REDACTED_SECRET] that happens to leave gliders in its wake (a moving gun!), we can crash two puffers together or use them to build a complex gun.

I've written a new search script specifically looking for **[REDACTED_SECRET]** (symmetry across the primary axis so they can travel straight down it). It is running 100,000 trials right now!

Meanwhile, the Reflector search and the brute-force collision search are still grinding in the background. We are attacking this from 3 different structural angles simultaneously!


