### 2026-08-16T01:17:52Z | agy | user | conversation 4abc6e66-08cd-4cb4-9377-f2da45e943a9

> Conversation with another gemini: I was once lucky enough to attend an MIT PhD defense, probably around 2008, about the turing completeness of sliding block puzzles. Remind me whose it was?
>
> You are likely thinking of Robert A. Hearn (often known as Bob Hearn).
>
> He defended his PhD thesis, titled "Games, Puzzles, and Computation", at MIT in 2006 (which aligns closely with your ~2008 memory). His work was co-advised by Erik Demaine and Gerald Jay Sussman.  
> Erik Demaine
> + 1
>
> In his thesis, Hearn introduced the framework of Nondeterministic Constraint Logic. He essentially proved that you can build functional mechanical computers out of sliding blocks (like the puzzle Rush Hour). By arranging blocks to act as logic gates (AND, OR, signals, and wires), he demonstrated that determining whether certain sliding block puzzles can be solved is computationally equivalent to solving any problem a computer can solve given bounded memory—proving them to be PSPACE-complete.  
> Wikipedia
> + 2
>
> He and Erik Demaine later expanded his dissertation into a well-known 2009 book also titled Games, Puzzles, and Computation.  
> Wikipedia
>
> relatedly, Conway's game of life is Turing-complete as shown by similar construction, right? who did that? Other cellular automata? life on a hexagonal grid
>
> Yes, you are exactly right about the construction method. In Conway’s Game of Life, Turing completeness is achieved by treating streams of moving "gliders" as electrical pulses (where the presence of a glider is a 1 and its absence is a 0). By colliding these glider streams in specific geometric configurations, you can simulate logic gates (AND, OR, NOT). Once you have universal logic gates and a way to route signals (glider paths) and store memory (using feedback loops or blocks), you can theoretically compute anything.  
> DROPS - Schloss Dagstuhl
> + 1
>
> Who proved it?
> The proof of Turing completeness in the Game of Life was a team effort that spanned decades:
>
> Bill Gosper (1970): The proof became possible when Gosper (another MIT legend) discovered the "Glider Gun," a configuration that infinitely generates gliders. This proved the Game of Life could have patterns that grow indefinitely, and provided the necessary "power source" for a computer.  
> Theorem of the Day
>
> John Conway, Elwyn Berlekamp, and Richard Guy (1982): John Conway and his colleagues formally published the proof that these glider collisions could act as universal logic gates in their definitive book Winning Ways for your Mathematical Plays.
>
> Paul Rendell (2000): While Conway proved it was theoretically possible, Rendell actually went ahead and constructed the first explicitly functioning Universal Turing Machine entirely within the Game of Life grid.
>
> Other Turing-Complete Cellular Automata
> The Game of Life is far from the only Turing-complete cellular automaton. Two other very famous examples include:
>
> Rule 110: A one-dimensional cellular automaton. Despite its extreme simplicity (a single row of cells updating based only on their immediate left and right neighbors), Matthew Cook proved in 2004 that it is Turing-complete.  
> Degenerate State
>
> Wireworld: Designed by Brian Silverman in [REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO], this 2D automaton was explicitly built to simulate electronic logic elements. It uses four states (Empty, Electron Head, Electron Tail, Conductor) and is frequently used by hobbyists to build complex computers, including machines that calculate prime numbers.
>
> Life on a Hexagonal Grid
> Adapting Conway's Life to a hexagonal grid (often referred to as "HexLife") is a classic recreational computing puzzle, but it comes with a geometric hurdle.
>
> In a standard square grid (the Moore neighborhood), a cell has 8 neighbors. In a standard flat-top hexagonal grid, a cell only has 6 edge neighbors. If you try to directly port Conway's famous B3/S23 rule (Born on 3, Survives on 2 or 3) to a 6-neighbor grid, the delicate balance between chaotic growth and isolated death falls apart.  
> CEUR-WS.org
>
> To make hexagonal grids interesting, mathematicians have taken two main approaches:
>
> Changing the Rules: Researchers like Carter Bays (who also pioneered 3D cellular automata) explored different birth and survival integer thresholds on standard hexagonal and pentagonal tessellations to find the "Goldilocks" zones that produce gliders and oscillators.  
> Wikipedia
>
> Expanding the Neighborhood: The most popular HexLife implementations keep the spirit of Conway's rules by expanding the neighborhood to 12 cells. By including the 6 immediate edge-sharing neighbors plus the 6 closest secondary neighbors (the ones sharing a vertex), you bring the math much closer to the complexity of the original Game of Life.
>
> So TonnetzLife expands the neighborhood in ways similar to Bays and others in two ways: allowing some amount of directional choice with the minor thirds, major thirds, and fifths axes, and by allowing the rules to reach the tone and semitone neighbors. I think even without those, replicating the 3-state beehive rules, I now have a glider gun in TonnetzLife. How close am I to proving Turing completeness, and would it be interesting from a recreational math perspective to do so? (don't worry, I'm planning to whether you call it interesting or not).
>
> Finding a glider gun in a custom cellular automaton is the "fire" moment of recreational computing—congratulations! That is a massive leap forward.
>
> Having a glider gun gives you your power supply and an infinite signal generator. You are roughly halfway to a formal proof of Turing completeness, but the second half is a rigorous engineering challenge. To cross the finish line, you essentially need to show that you can wire these gliders together to build the primitive components of a universal computer.
>
> 1
> Characterize Glider Kinematics
> Establish the strict timing and geometry of your signals
> You need to map exactly how your gliders move. What is their period? What is their step size? If they move along the minor third or fifth axes of your Tonnetz grid, you need to mathematically define their phase and spatial displacement over time so you can predict exact collision frames.
>
> 2
> Engineer Routing and Delays
> Control the flow of information
> A computer needs wires. You must find stable "eater" blocks that can cleanly absorb a glider without destroying themselves. From there, you need to find reflectors—arrangements of blocks or precise glider-on-glider collisions that change a glider's trajectory by exactly 60 or 120 degrees (depending on your grid's geometry) without cascading into chaos.
>
> 3
> Synthesize a Universal Logic Gate
> The core requirement for computation
> By intentionally crashing glider streams into each other at specific phases and angles, you need to create a universal boolean gate (like NAND or NOR), or a combination of AND, OR, and NOT gates. For example, if two gliders collide and annihilate each other, that can function as a NOT gate (one signal prevents the other from passing).
>
> 4
> Establish a Memory State
> Proving tape equivalence
> Turing machines require unbounded memory. You need to demonstrate that a glider can toggle a static formation between two stable states (a 1 and a 0), and that another glider can non-destructively "read" that state.
>
> From a recreational math perspective, this is an incredibly compelling project. The Tonnetz lattice isn't just a geometric quirk; it is inherently a topological map of harmonic space. If your gliders are traversing minor thirds or major fifths, you are essentially computing via Neo-Riemannian music theory. That topological mapping—where spatial distance equals harmonic distance—adds a completely unique flavor to the automaton that traditional square or hexagonal grids lack.
>
> Once you have your glider kinematics locked down, manual testing of collisions will quickly become tedious. Framing the collision geometries as a constraint satisfaction problem can be highly effective here. You can define the initial states of two converging gliders and your desired output (e.g., mutual annihilation or a clean right-angle reflection), and let a solver computationally sweep the bounding box for valid positional offsets.


