### 2026-08-16T21:44:32Z | agy | agy | conversation 4abc6e66-08cd-4cb4-9377-f2da45e943a9

When I say "via collision," I'm referring to the method we use to mechanically synthesize the [REDACTED_PERSON] Gun in the first place.

In almost all cellular automata, a [REDACTED_PERSON] Gun is an incredibly complex, delicate oscillator that happens to shed a moving piece of itself (a glider) at the end of its periodic cycle. Because these structures are so large and precise, the odds of one spontaneously forming from random static noise are astronomically low.

Instead of waiting millions of generations hoping one forms by pure chance, we take an engineering approach. In Phase 6 of our script, we take a known **[REDACTED_PERSON]** and we shoot a **[REDACTED_PERSON]** directly into it at a precisely calculated angle and sub-pixel offset. 

When the glider impacts the block, the resulting explosion of debris creates a temporary pocket of chaos. The script lets this chaos evolve. In 99% of cases, the debris either fades to nothing, or stabilizes into static junk. But at the *exact right offset* (like the `[REDACTED_SECRET]` offset we found in [REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]), the collision perfectly shatters the block into a self-sustaining oscillating core. This core becomes a permanent engine that infinitely spits out new gliders. 

This is mechanically similar to how the famous "[REDACTED_SECRET]" was discovered in Conway's Game of Life—not by random chance, but by deliberately crashing two smaller moving blocks (Queen Bee shuttles) into each other to synthesize the larger gun structure!


